hello everyone and welcome to another  Bitcoin protocol explained video in the  previous videos we covered the basics up  basic ideas behind Bitcoin in the  abstract we covered the introduction and  we cover the transactions section in  this video we're going to cover the  timestamps server the problem with  transactions is we we have a way to do  them but we have no way as of yet to  confirm that that the transaction itself  was not double spent nor the previous  transactions in this chain of  transactions we can confirm that the  person owns or at least owned you know  this Bitcoin but we have no idea if they  went to multiple parties and spent that  coin multiple times so here's the  beginning of that solution and that is a  timestamp server so let's go ahead and  hit this short section the solution we  propose begins with a timestamp server a  timestamp server works by taking a hash  of a block of items to be time-stamped  and widely publishing the hash such as  in a newspaper or a Usenet post the  timestamp proves that the data must have  existed at the time obviously in order  to get into the hash each timestamp  includes the previous timestamp in its  hash forming a chain with each  additional timestamp reinforcing the  ones before it so this should be you  know pretty familiar to you after going  through the abstract video the idea here  is that we can timestamp transactions  each time we do it or do a transaction  right and it basically is going to  enforce the previous stance right  because it's like more agreement on the  previous stamp so if you think about  like any transfer you've done in Bitcoin  you've probably seen that it takes  something like three confirmations to be  validated so this means that your  transfer has been agreed on the another  block is made and then another block is  made and so on depending on how many  confirmations are required and by this  point you've solidified your transaction  in the Bitcoin blockchain and it's also  you know widely agreed-upon the problem  is however what if someone decides to  take the chain  and just edit the chain real quick and  pass it along  then what so sadly as we've resolved one  problem we've presented ourselves with  another luckily there is a solution and  you probably already know that solution  if you've paid attention or watched the  abstract video so in the next video  we're going to be covering this notion  of proof of work we did kind of discuss  it already but we will continue it as  it's broken down further in the next  section  as always hopefully everybody is  following along well if you're confused  or have any questions or comments feel  free to make them make them below  otherwise stay tuned for the next video  thanks for watching and thanks for all  the subscriptions
 

